    "report": "Phone numbers written with hyphens are now recognised as contact details, and the change is committed on `main` as `6ced78a`. I did not push.\n\n**What changed**\n- The phone pattern is now `/\\+?\\d(?:[ .-]?\\d){8,}/` in both places that hold one:\n  - `Messaging/Domain/Conversation/ContactDetails.ts`, where matches are held back as `[hidden]`.\n  - `Reviews/Domain/StayReview/ReviewRules.ts`, where a comment or reply is refused as `CONTACT_DETAILS`.\n- ADR 63 says a change to what counts as a phone number is made in each context that holds one, so I changed both rather than sharing a helper.\n- Specs in both contexts now cover `06-12-34-56-78` and `+33-6-12-34-56-78`. They also check that `2026-12-01` and a double hyphen such as `06--12-34-56-78` are not phone numbers.\n\n**Verification**\n- Unit tests: 1052 passed. Before the change it was 1049, so the three new cases pass.\n- Messaging, Reviews and conversation e2e specs: 139 passed.\n- `tsc`, `eslint` and `prettier --check .` are clean.\n- The full `yarn test` run exhausts the Node heap on this machine, so I ran the unit suite and the related e2e specs separately. Other e2e specs were not run.\n- I did not start a server, so port 4471 was not used.\n\n**Choices and unclear points**\n- You asked me to commit on the current branch, which is `main`. I followed that instruction and did not create a feature branch.\n- The rule as written also matches a date followed by a space and more digits, for example `2026-12-01 2026`. It has 12 digits in one chain, so it counts as a phone number. The spec only fixes the plain date case.\n- I left the ADRs unchanged. ADR 55 and ADR 63 describe phone numbers in general terms, so they still hold.",
